01|ols-example|a-m2-p2-ols|examples/a-m2-p2-ols/main.rs|19|27||e-11 OK|assert!|verdict\(|The example measures the largest gap from the golden file, asserts it stays under one e minus eight, and prints the contract line through verdict.|Now cargo runs it live. Intercept minus 5780.83, slope 49.69, and the contract line turns green: the gap is far inside the bound.
02|cv-example|a-m2-p2-cv|examples/a-m2-p2-cv/main.rs|20|29||e-13 OK|assert!|verdict\(|The cross-validation example takes the larger of the two RMSE gaps from the golden file, asserts it is within one e minus six, and prints the contract line.|Cargo runs it live. The plain line scores 394.31, the species model 376.64, and the contract line turns green.
03|unit-tests|a-m2-p2-ols|tests/p2.rs|32|47|cargo test --locked --offline --test p2|test result: ok.|p2_json|<=|Two Rust tests check the fitted coefficients within one e minus eight and the cross-validation error within one e minus six, both against the golden file.|Cargo test runs them live: five tests pass, and the result line turns green.
05|design-species|a-m2-p2-cv|src/p2.rs|49|58|||DUMMIES|f64::from|The species design matrix has the flipper length in column zero, then one dummy column per species, set to one when the row matches.|Cargo runs the cross-validation that uses it: the species model scores 376.64 and the contract line turns green.
06|read-folds|a-m2-p2-cv|src/p2.rs|77|91|||skip\(1\)|parse|read_folds skips the header, splits each line on the comma, and parses the fold column into an integer per row.|Because the fold file is committed, cargo prints the same 394.31 and 376.64 on every machine, and the contract line turns green.
07|cv-rmse|a-m2-p2-cv|src/p2.rs|112|128|||predict_species|pred\[i\]|cv_rmse loops over the folds, trains on the rest, predicts the held-out rows, and returns the pooled RMSE from regression_metrics.|Cargo runs it on the five fixed folds: 394.31 for the plain line, 376.64 for the species model, and the contract line turns green.
08|fit-linfa|a-m2-p2-cv|src/p2.rs|62|75|||LinearRegression::new|sse /|fit_linfa hands the design matrix to linfa's LinearRegression, and regression_metrics turns the squared errors into an RMSE and an r squared.|Cargo runs the cross-validation through them, and the contract line turns green.
09|p2-json|a-m2-p2-ols|src/p2.rs|134|147|cargo test --locked --offline --test p2|test result: ok.|ols_ndarray|fit_linfa|p2_json assembles the golden-file layout: the line fit from ols_ndarray, the species model from fit_linfa, and the metrics.|Cargo test checks that layout against the golden file: five tests pass, and the result line turns green.
10|mutants|a-m2-p2-ols|tests/p2.rs|49|56|cargo test --locked --offline --test p2|test result: ok.|1 \+ i % 2|assert!|A mutant test breaks the fold assignment on purpose and asserts that the cross-validation error moves, so the check can fail when the code is wrong.|Cargo runs the two mutant tests: both pass, which proves the golden check refuses wrong folds, and the result line turns green.
04|ols|a-m2-p2-ols|src/p2.rs|41|47||contract: ads-rust-|let slope|my - slope|Here is ols_ndarray in Rust. The slope is the cross product, sxy, divided by the sum of squares, sxx. Then the intercept is the mean of y minus slope times the mean of x, and the function returns the pair.|Now cargo runs it live. Intercept minus 5780.83, slope 49.69, and the contract line turns green: the maximum difference from the golden file is within 1e-8.
